From 4a3f5980656dff5528aed9eb972d00f6ae8b801a Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: ayiejosh <132535860+ayiejosh@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Fri, 16 Oct 2026 15:11:58 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 122/269] gallium/pvr: the control stream builder, reusing
 csbgen's packet layouts

The first piece of the command stream: a writer that reserves slots in a buffer the
GPU will execute, with the packet layouts coming from csbgen - the same generator the
Vulkan driver uses - rather than being restated here.

  csb: packet at the right slot (word 0xffff0000), readback matches the generated
       pack/unpack, 4096 bytes fills exactly, one more word refused
  csb: PASS

csbgen gives a field struct plus a pack AND an unpack per packet, generated from
csbgen/rogue/*.xml. That unpacker is what makes the check meaningful: the packet is
written into the buffer, then read back out of the buffer with the generated reader,
which is reading the memory the GPU would read. dep_csbgen carries the include path,
and the helpers need __pvr_address_type/__pvr_get_address/__pvr_make_address defined
first - device addresses are plain uint64_t here, so they pass through.

Overflow is refused rather than truncated. This is not defensive habit: the GPU
executes this stream, so overrunning it corrupts whatever follows in the buffer,
silently, at draw time, on hardware. A userspace NULL is a far better failure. The
check exercises the boundary in both directions - filling the buffer exactly must
succeed, and one word more must fail without changing the stream.

A test assumption worth recording, because it was mine and not the writer's:
PBESTATE_STATE_WORD0 carries only the LOW word of a split framebuffer address, so a
36-bit address does not survive one word. The first version of the check asserted the
full address round-tripped and failed against correct code. It now compares the
generated pack/unpack round-trip, which is what this component can actually vouch for.

Not done: nothing emits a control stream in anger. There is no job description and no
SUBMIT_JOBS, so this is a verified component with no caller yet.
